WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.860
Cyber incidents create continuity problems when systems, visibility, communications,

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:07.405
identity, or trust can no longer be relied upon.

00:00:07.405 --> 00:00:09.895
Ransomware may disable HMIs and servers.

00:00:09.895 --> 00:00:12.115
Destructive activity may affect configurations.

00:00:12.115 --> 00:00:14.460
Loss of remote access may isolate support,

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:17.630
while loss of monitoring can make continued operation unsafe.

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:20.880
Incident response and continuity teams should share one operating picture:

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:25.420
affected assets, process state, safe operating limits, evidence, containment options,

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:26.720
and recovery priorities.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:28.560
Cybersecurity leads investigation;

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:32.300
operations and engineering determine physical consequence and safe action.

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:34.040
Containment choices require care.

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:38.640
Disconnecting a network path may stop unwanted access but also remove alarms, control,

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:40.400
redundancy, or historian data.

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:43.990
Decisions should follow preplanned authority and consider whether a local fallback or

00:00:43.990 --> 00:00:45.695
controlled shutdown is available.

00:00:45.695 --> 00:00:49.740
A safe shutdown may be the best continuity action when process integrity or visibility

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:50.840
cannot be trusted.

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:55.280
Plans should define shutdown prerequisites, communications, staffing, restart conditions,

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:58.270
and the assets that must remain protected while production is stopped.

00:00:58.270 --> 00:01:01.015
Do not rush restoration because business pressure is high.

00:01:01.015 --> 00:01:04.680
Validate backups, images, controller logic, configurations, credentials,

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:06.555
and dependencies before reconnecting.

00:01:06.555 --> 00:01:09.855
Preserve evidence and determine whether the cause remains present.

00:01:09.855 --> 00:01:12.440
Recovery from a compromised state can reintroduce the incident.

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:16.330
Communicate clearly with leadership, employees, vendors, customers, regulators,

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:18.065
and public authorities as required.

00:01:18.065 --> 00:01:22.600
After recovery, update process maps, backups, fallback procedures, security controls,

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:23.540
and exercises.

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:27.175
Continuity succeeds when the incident is contained without sacrificing safe and

00:01:27.175 --> 00:01:28.520
trustworthy operations.
